\chapter{The Socius System}\label{sec:methodology}

    \section{Software Overview}

    The software system, namely Socius, will be a chatbot designed to assist users in improving their pragmatic competence through dialogue-based training and feedback mechanisms utilizing the APAM Framework. The chatbot is designed to utilize the Gemini API as its core conversational engine. All interactions and feedback are processed and evaluated against rule-based patterns drawn from pragmatics research as mentioned previously in this document. 

    \section{Software Objectives}

    % 
    The objectives of the Socius chatbot system are as follows:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item To integrate a user profile and session-tracking database, enabling tracking of pragmatic competence metrics and individual progress across multiple sessions.
        \item To provide users a selection of predefined social scenarios that they may select from, which then allows them to engage in the chatbot's dialogue-based training and feedback mechanisms. 
        \item To utilize the Gemini API to generate responses that are contextually relevant and research-based in regards to pragmatics, ensuring that the chatbot adheres to Grice's maxims and implicatures.
        \item To implement a user interface that facilitates the conversation, similar to text messaging applications, that will allow users to interact with the chatbot through text input thereby receiving responses from the chatbot.
    \end{itemize}

    \section{Software Scope and Limitations}

    \hl{The system establishes a backend database that stores user profiles, conversation transcripts, and metrics related to pragmatic competence. Authentication requires minimal user information—specifically an email, password, and age—ensuring that external user data, such as social media profiles, are not collected. This database hosts the conversation logs per user session, allowing the model to longitudinally analyze the user's pragmatic competence and track their progress through an interactive Progress Dashboard.

    The system provides a fixed set of predefined conversational scenarios. Users select from these scenarios to practice, with contexts covering various academic, professional, and social situations relevant to young adults. To maintain targeted pedagogical goals and ensure alignment with the established theoretical frameworks, the system is strictly limited to these predefined scenarios and does not allow for dynamic scenario generation or user-generated contexts.

    To generate contextually relevant and pragmatically appropriate dialogue, the system leverages the Gemini API. Specifically, it utilizes the gemini-3-flash-preview model under a Tier 1 paid plan to balance high rate limits with advanced reasoning capabilities. The architecture employs configured Thinking Budgets to adapt to different agent roles: a lower latency setting for the AI Partner's naturalistic roleplay, and a higher reasoning budget for the AI Mentor's deep pragmatic analysis. Furthermore, feedback is strictly enforced via JSON Schema Controlled Generation, dynamically evaluating the user's adherence to Grice's Maxims and sociolinguistic norms based on the transcript, rather than relying on static text references.

    The user interface is designed to resemble common text messaging applications, presenting conversational exchanges and feedback in a familiar layout. The scope encompasses essential UI elements for text-based interaction, interactive data visualization (via Chart.js) for the user's progress dashboard, and basic personalizations such as dark mode and background customization. However, it does not include voice input/output capabilities or advanced 3D graphical elements.}

    \subsection*{Limitations}

    \hl{Relying on a text-only LLM modality inherently limits the system's capacity to train paralinguistic or non-verbal communication skills. Additionally, end-user testing revealed that the AI Partner's responses can occasionally become too lengthy or dramatic, which breaks the realism and pacing of casual texting. The system is also bound by the constraints of its infrastructure and third-party APIs. The reliance on the Supabase Free Tier for database management and authentication limits long-term scalability and concurrent user capacity. Finally, the software proved vulnerable to prompt injection if a user intentionally stresses the system's instructions, and it is susceptible to occasional slow response times or dropped context due to network errors and high API traffic demand.}


    {\section{Architectural Design}}
    The architectural design of Socius is structured to facilitate effective and pragmatic interactions between the user and the chatbot. 
    As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:socius_architecture}, the design is composed of several key components that interact with one another in order to process user input, manage 
    conversation flow, analyze pragmatics, and generate appropriate responses and feedback.

    Input received via \textbf{User Interface} is sent to the \textbf{Conversation Manager}, which is made of two subcomponents: \textbf{Input Processing} and \textbf{Conversation Flow Manager}. The Conversation Flow Manager oversees scenario generation, pulled from a database of possible scenarios and scenario progression. This also takes care of saving interaction state, and context tracking and remembering. Next, the data goes to the \textbf{Pragmatic Analyzer}, which then evaluates the input based on Grice’s Maxims and Implicature rules. This will help the AI determine the appropriate response to the conversation. If the scenario is still ongoing, the response is then provided by Gemini AI and returned to the user to maintain conversation flow while also saving the messages into the conversation log database. Once the conversation ends, information from both the conversation logs and the user’s profile will then be used for \textbf{Feedback Generation}, delivered by Gemini, that may help the user improve their social communication skills. The user may also ask questions related to the given feedback in case they need clarification or further explanation.

