    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/expert_evaluate.png}
    \caption{Expert Scenario Evaluation Rubric}
    \label{fig:expert_evaluation_rubric}
\end{figure}


This allowed the expert to evaluate each scenario in an isolated manner. The research proponents also facilitated an online meeting to formally introduce the proposed frameworks in the research, to break down the structure of our research and scenario design, and to clarify any questions that the expert may have had. The expert's ratings and qualitative feedback were then compiled and analyzed to identify any necessary revisions to the list. 
Considering this, the comments from the expert were generally positive and that the frameworks were valid. The expert also provided suggestions to change certain details in a scenario that would make the scenario feel more controlled (e.g. keeping a scenario one-on-one rather than having a group approach, changing chatbot role to be more relatable to a student, etc.) as it may deter from the scenario's intended pragmatic conflict.
These suggestions were then implemented and clarified on the face-to-face meeting with the expert, where we let the expert demo the system and experience the scenarios first-hand. The expert had then fully validated and approved the frameworks that went into training pragmatical competence.

\subsection{End-User Testing}

Currently, End-User testing is on-going and is being conducted with a sample of approximately 15 young adults within the target demographic. The testing is being conducted in an online environment wherein the users can access the chatbot whenever they want, and they are asked to complete at least 1 conversation per day with the chatbot prior to answering the daily post-interaction survey.
The participants and the research team have created a Discord server where the users are facilitated in their use of the chatbot except for the conversation with the chatbots itself. This is to ensure that the users are not lost in the chatbot's features and that they are not influenced by the research team in their interactions with the chatbot.
